Response: {"belief": "The two-year-old boy's distal femoral fracture prompted evaluation. Skeletal survey findings—cranial/facial hyperostosis, sclerosed ribs, clavicular hyperostosis, hemivertebral scoliosis, femoral diaphyseal hyperostosis with stress fractures, hand dysplasia, and optic canal narrowing—support a diffuse genetic bone disorder rather than focal trauma. These findings strongly support genetic bone fragility (0.80) and sclerosing skeletal dysplasia (0.65), while refuting non-accidental trauma (0.10), metabolic bone disease (0.08), pathological fracture (0.02), and classic osteogenesis imperfecta (0.10). Laxity and motor delay are nonspecific and insufficient for classic OI. Consensus favors an underlying sclerosing skeletal dysplasia / genetic bone fragility; genetic and metabolic labs remain pending for confirmation."}
